  "articleBody": "It's super common for trim to look out of whack after you've had new flooring installed, isn't it? What happens is that new floor heights can change the clearances around doors and walls, making your previously perfect trim now seem to float, gape, or even get snagged. We've seen this countless times. The trick isn't just to jam the old trim back in or try to fill huge gaps with caulk; that often just looks messy. You really need to inspect how the new floor elevation has affected the fit. Sometimes, you might need entirely new baseboards cut to the correct height, or door casings might need to be adjusted or even replaced to accommodate the change. A frequent mistake we see is people focusing solely on the trim without considering if the underlying door or window frame also needs adjustment because of the new floor. It's a sequence thing—the trim needs to fit perfectly *after* any other elements are stable. If you're looking at significant gaps, or if the trim around your doors is now interfering with their operation, it's definitely time to think about professional help. Getting it done right means ensuring a clean, seamless look that truly finishes the room, not just a temporary fix. Don't hesitate to reach out if you're in the Denver area and need a hand!",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
